WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:01.400
- She started using the

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:04.051
printing out transcripts of Khan Academy

00:00:04.051 --> 00:00:06.560
and giving him these sheets,

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.660
these stacks of paper when
she visited him in prison.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.250
and through just the transcripts

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:14.860
Jason was able to start realizing that he,

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:17.500
you know in school he felt weak in math

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:19.460
but now he's like I can learn this stuff

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:22.410
and his sentence was was taken down

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:24.510
from 30 years to still 15 years

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:28.060
so now in his mid-30s
he's released from prison

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:29.220
he's feeling more confident

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:30.550
about his knowledge.

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:32.290
He goes to community college

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:34.180
and then he's able to
use Khan Academy properly

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:35.730
with the videos and the exercises

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:37.480
what a lot of y'all are familiar with

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:39.620
and then he decides

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:41.660
he wants to transfer
to a four-year college.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:44.240
He uses the SAT prep
that we do in partnership

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:48.560
with the College Board and
he starts doing quite well

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:50.490
and I mean this is what's
really mind-blowing

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:54.670
at age 35 he was able to
transfer to Stanford University

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:56.610
as a computer science student
and that was the Jason,

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.850
and by the end of the story
we were all tearing up

00:00:58.850 --> 00:00:59.683
this was as powerful,

00:00:59.683 --> 00:01:00.537
and then I asked him I was like,

00:01:00.537 --> 00:01:01.727
"Hey when everyone leaves

00:01:01.727 --> 00:01:04.270
"can I take my phone
out and just video you

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:06.247
"because I think your story,"

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.890
obviously it's something
that we love to share

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:11.950
in forums like this

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:15.840
when we're pleading for people to realize

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.920
that hey Kahn Academy needs your help

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:20.370
but it's also an inspiring story.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:21.530
I think we all have days

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:22.363
where we're like,

00:01:22.363 --> 00:01:24.597
"Oh things could be a little bit easier

00:01:24.597 --> 00:01:26.810
"or I had that setback or that setback."

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:27.890
But when you when you hear a story

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:30.880
like Jason's you realize
that most of our setbacks

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:32.640
are fairly small in comparison

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.480
to what he's what he has gone through

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.690
and you see how far far he's gone.

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:40.630
And he still has setbacks

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:41.990
but it's amazing how positive he is.

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:43.840
I mean you know when
you have a prison record

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:45.330
it's still hard to get internships

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:46.330
it's still hard to get jobs

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:48.260
even if you have Stanford on your resume.

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:52.510
So I think it's always good,

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:53.780
Thanksgiving was last week,

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:56.260
but I'm still kind of in
the Thanksgiving mode.

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:58.960
I think we all need to really appreciate

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.170
how good most of us have it.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:04.430
And Jason appreciates how good he has it,

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:07.150
even though he's had a tough beginning.

