00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:43.112
So what is your advice for
when you can't get your

00:05:43.112 --> 00:05:45.150
SAT score up?

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:47.090
- All right, SAT score.

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:48.180
So I think there's a couple of things

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:51.080
so before even thinking about
how to get the SAT score up,

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:55.580
it's just to remind and
we made a video about

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:57.040
de-stressing the SAT.

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:59.640
First appreciate that you
are not your SAT score.

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:02.350
I think especially when
you are in the later stages

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:04.150
of high school and you're in this period

00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:05.160
where you're applying to college

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:06.090
and I know that's is happening

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:07.680
to a lot of students right now

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:09.010
or maybe there's a lot of juniors

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:10.460
who are starting to
think about the process

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:11.610
for next year.

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:15.150
It can seem like your whole
identity is going to be defined

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:18.300
by your grades, your extracurriculars,

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:21.130
your SAT score, where you go
to college et cetera et cetera,

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:22.680
everyone's comparing.

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:25.630
And that's not the
healthiest thing in the world

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:28.050
and we've all been there but I think

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:32.400
what you're going to
realize is over the broader

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.370
sweep of your life that these are all

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:36.790
just very minor data points.

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:39.430
We have people who've, billionaires,

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:43.530
some of whom who've
donated to Khan Academy

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:47.800
and their SAT scores
were not so impressive,

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:49.610
but they're very impressive people,

00:06:49.610 --> 00:06:50.600
they're very entrepreneurial,

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:51.840
they were able to go great things,

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:54.823
and they might've just not
been for whatever reason,

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:59.910
you know, great test takers or
whatever you want to call it.

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:02.660
So that's my first foundational is like

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:04.830
you are not your SAT scores,
you are not your grades,

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:07.730
you are not defined by
where you go to college.

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:11.330
And likewise, I know people
with very good data points

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:13.260
who, you know, for various reasons

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:15.090
have had to struggle in various ways,

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:17.890
so, once again, that's the disclaimer.

00:07:17.890 --> 00:07:18.750
- That's a great tip.

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:22.390
- Yes, and it's easy to pretend

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:23.310
that like,

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:27.050
I'm beyond all of this and I
have to remind it to myself

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:31.150
sometimes that sometimes
you visit a friend

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:33.420
with a massive house and you're
like, "Oh, this is nice,"

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:36.713
no, I'm not defined by the
square footage of my house.

00:07:38.797 --> 00:07:43.797
But my advice for you for
SAT or really any test,

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:48.340
one, there's direct advice,

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:49.850
we have the official SAT practice,

00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:51.630
that we're partnered
with the College Board

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:54.370
and we have many efficacy studies

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:56.000
that have shown that pretty much

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:59.320
every hour that you're able
to engage on the platform,

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:02.320
you are going to grow
and there are better ways

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:05.460
to use things like our
official SAT practice.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:08.250
I would recommend taking
as many practice tests

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:09.400
as you can.

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:11.130
When you take the practice tests,

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:13.690
try to simulate real testing conditions

00:08:13.690 --> 00:08:15.380
as well as possible.

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:17.610
After you've taken the test
and you've graded your test,

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:21.990
look at what you got wrong
and go back to those questions

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:26.480
and make sure that you
understand how they work.

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:28.040
A lot of people when they see a test,

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:29.580
especially like a test like the SAT,

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:30.413
they're just like, "Oh, this is hard,

00:08:30.413 --> 00:08:32.170
"there's just going to be
questions I don't know,"

00:08:32.170 --> 00:08:34.370
but it's worth appreciating that the SAT,

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:36.120
especially the SAT over
the last several years,

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:39.380
it really doesn't over
anything that isn't taught

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:42.160
outside of algebra two or
outside of the tenth grade.

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:43.640
So if you feel pretty comfortable

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:44.737
and that you shouldn't
look at anything and say,

00:08:44.737 --> 00:08:45.617
"Oh this is some type of

00:08:45.617 --> 00:08:48.620
"mysterious quantum physics calculus,"

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:49.850
nothing like that's on the SAT,

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:51.980
it's all stuff that you should see

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:53.820
by the time you're in tenth grade.

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:56.670
So you should feel confident
that you can do any problem,

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:58.920
reflect on what you weren't able to do.

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:00.040
Always work on the stuff

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:01.880
that you find maybe most challenging,

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:03.130
sometimes it's a temptation for us

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:04.467
to work on the stuff that's easy.

00:09:04.467 --> 00:09:05.680
- Yeah, definitely
- Easy for us.

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:07.270
And what I'm describing
and this would be true

00:09:07.270 --> 00:09:11.130
if you are preparing for
some type of a sport as well,

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:13.320
practice what you're not good at,

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:15.440
try to simulate real
conditions when possible,

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:18.930
do that weak point
training and I would say

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:23.093
rather than binge prepare, just
do eight hours all at once,

00:09:24.224 --> 00:09:26.270
if you have enough time,
let's says you're a month away

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:31.270
from the SAT, try to
put in 20 minutes a day.

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:34.400
If some days you're able to
put in more that's great.

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:36.080
But if you're able to
do that regular practice

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:39.400
and then maybe once a week
do a full form practice exam,

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:41.947
I think you're going to
be in pretty good shape.

00:09:41.947 --> 00:09:44.060
And I've already said this advice,

00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:46.780
we were on Instagram, as a pre-livestream.

00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:49.223
And I've actually been meditating a ton,

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:52.540
especially the last year and a half,

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:54.600
it's helped me just with
all of the different things

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:59.253
in my life, with family
and work and raising money.

00:10:01.390 --> 00:10:03.573
You can sometimes just
get really stressed.

00:10:05.874 --> 00:10:07.010
At first when you start meditating

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:10.060
and you start maybe five,
10 minutes at a time,

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:11.800
now I do 30 minutes every morning.

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:12.720
You're like, what am I doing?

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:14.650
Your brain starts like,
you're wasting your time,

00:10:14.650 --> 00:10:15.840
you have so much to do.

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:18.630
But slowly your brain starts to still

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:20.530
and when you're in that kind of a mode

00:10:20.530 --> 00:10:22.090
everything becomes easier.

00:10:22.090 --> 00:10:23.750
And ideally when you take the SAT

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:25.040
you're in that type of a mode,

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:27.620
you've gotten your sleep,
you've got your food

00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:28.883
and you're just ready.

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:34.654
And you do what you can and
the chips fall where they do.

00:10:34.654 --> 00:10:36.060
One of the things that,

00:10:38.461 --> 00:10:41.340
I've been interested in a
lot of Easter philosophy

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:43.140
ever since we did some of
the world history content

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:46.370
on Khan Academy and in Vedic philosophy,

00:10:46.370 --> 00:10:50.550
in the Hindu philosophy,
there's these notions

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:54.230
of aspects of the universe
including in work.

00:10:54.230 --> 00:10:56.310
And there's sawthwik work

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:58.380
which is work that is true work

00:10:58.380 --> 00:11:00.430
that you do because you think it is right

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:03.263
but you're not fixated on the outcome.

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:07.630
Regosic work is work where
you are somewhat fixated

00:11:07.630 --> 00:11:08.463
on the outcome.

00:11:08.463 --> 00:11:10.690
I'm going to do this because
we're going to raise $5000

00:11:10.690 --> 00:11:12.140
and if we don't I'm going
to be really disappointed,

00:11:12.140 --> 00:11:13.690
which I will be.

00:11:13.690 --> 00:11:15.950
Or if I don't do this I'm
going to get into trouble,

00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:17.060
therefore I do it.

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:19.090
And then you have themosic work which is

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:20.670
I'm doing because someone's forcing me

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:22.880
I don't really want to do
it, I'm dragging my feet,

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:24.450
I'm not really into it.

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:28.250
And I think most of us are
kind of in this regosic mode,

00:11:28.250 --> 00:11:29.810
most of our lives, we're like,

00:11:29.810 --> 00:11:32.460
I need a good SAT score
and if I don't get that

00:11:32.460 --> 00:11:34.930
I'm going to be miserable
and I'm going to suffer.

00:11:34.930 --> 00:11:38.260
And as Buddha says, "Suffering
is all caused by attachment,"

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:40.020
especially attachment to outcome.

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:41.917
So I remind myself every day

00:11:41.917 --> 00:11:43.990
and it definitely de-stresses me

00:11:43.990 --> 00:11:46.820
and anyone preparing for a
test or anything in life,

00:11:46.820 --> 00:11:48.593
try to take that sathwik attitude.

00:11:49.780 --> 00:11:51.740
Definitely put work into
it, put energy in it,

00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:54.470
do what you think is right
but don't be obsessed

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:56.680
with the outcome, the
outcome is what it is.

00:11:56.680 --> 00:11:57.670
If you do what you think is right

00:11:57.670 --> 00:11:59.740
the chips fall where
they do and then not only

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:01.100
will you find yourself less stressed,

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:03.190
but you'll probably have
better outcomes as well

00:12:03.190 --> 00:12:05.533
when you're not obsessed with them.

00:12:05.533 --> 00:12:07.890
It's a long answer, I got
a little bit more preachy

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:09.410
than I.

00:12:09.410 --> 00:12:11.246
- Yeah but super helpful and
great things to think about

00:12:11.246 --> 00:12:12.790
even like passing SATs.
- You're very flattering

00:12:12.790 --> 00:12:14.230
Lizzie, yes, yes.

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:15.063
- Even just regularly--

00:12:15.063 --> 00:12:19.158
- No, what I just said
applies well beyond the SATs

00:12:19.158 --> 00:12:20.880
and I still work.

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:21.970
Let's see other people.

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:24.540
Monica Kahn, maybe we're related,

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:26.070
thank you for your donation.

00:12:26.070 --> 00:12:27.760
Rocky Kirk, thank you.

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:28.593
- Thank you so much.

00:12:28.593 --> 00:12:30.310
- Suyog Chandramouli, thank you

00:12:30.310 --> 00:12:32.240
and twee dam, thank you so much

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:34.173
for your donations.

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:36.143
Let's see.
