00:12:36.143 --> 00:12:37.970
there was a funny comment,

00:12:37.970 --> 00:12:40.920
so when I hear Sal's
voice I want to do math.

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:43.094
- That's great, go do math.

00:12:43.094 --> 00:12:45.730
- Yes, that's awesome.

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:47.620
Another question from YouTube,

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:49.970
how did you get the idea
to start Khan Academy?

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:54.310
- Well I'll tell the story,
I'll give the short version

00:12:54.310 --> 00:12:56.503
of it.

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:00.960
From a very early age I was
actually very fascinated

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:03.640
by education even in undergrad

00:13:03.640 --> 00:13:06.140
when I was a CS major, a math major,

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:08.330
I was always fascinated by
how computers could be used

00:13:08.330 --> 00:13:10.840
to maybe help teach people
and I had some projects there.

00:13:10.840 --> 00:13:12.910
But I ended up working in tech,

00:13:12.910 --> 00:13:14.870
I ended up working
eventually at a hedge fund,

00:13:14.870 --> 00:13:17.820
which is really just a fancy
word for an investment firm

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:19.343
that has more flexibility.

00:13:20.550 --> 00:13:24.160
And while I was doing that,
right after my wedding,

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:25.560
my 12 year old cousin, Nadia,

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:26.620
it just came out in conversation

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:28.650
that she was having trouble in math,

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:30.350
so I offered to tutor her remotely,

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:32.450
I was in Boston, she was in New Orleans.

00:13:32.450 --> 00:13:34.053
So I start working with her,

00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:37.240
it was actually unit conversion
she was having trouble with.

00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:40.620
She got through that, she
got caught up with her class,

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:42.030
a little ahead of her class.

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:44.340
At that point she became
what I call a tiger cousin

00:13:44.340 --> 00:13:46.287
and I called up her school and I said,

00:13:46.287 --> 00:13:48.351
"I think Nadia should
retake that placement test."

00:13:48.351 --> 00:13:49.184
And they said, "Who are you?"

00:13:49.184 --> 00:13:50.017
And I said, "I'm her cousin."

00:13:50.017 --> 00:13:52.700
And they let her and
then she was actually put

00:13:52.700 --> 00:13:55.200
into an advanced math class
so I was a little bit hooked,

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:57.510
this was 15 years ago.

00:13:57.510 --> 00:14:00.480
And then I started tutoring
her younger brothers

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:03.070
and word spreads around my
family that free tutoring

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:06.250
is going on so I wax
tutoring like 10, 15 cousins.

00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:08.050
And then I thought, hey
these are great guinea pigs

00:14:08.050 --> 00:14:10.360
for me test some of my ideas out on

00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:13.920
like software and so I
wrote some software for them

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:15.270
from that practice problems.

00:14:15.270 --> 00:14:16.810
'Cause I saw all them, their main issue

00:14:16.810 --> 00:14:19.190
is they had gaps, a lot of them
they were trying their best,

00:14:19.190 --> 00:14:20.430
and they were all A or B students,

00:14:20.430 --> 00:14:22.490
but the reason why they're
having trouble in algebra

00:14:22.490 --> 00:14:24.360
wasn't that algebra was difficult,

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:25.770
it wasn't that why weren't bright,

00:14:25.770 --> 00:14:28.020
it's that they might've
been a little bit shaky

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:29.630
on negative numbers or a little shaky

00:14:29.630 --> 00:14:31.770
on dividing decimals from 5th grade.

00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:34.730
And so I was their tutor
to fill in those gaps

00:14:34.730 --> 00:14:36.740
but also I wanted to give them practice

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:38.600
so that they could get that feedback.

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:40.100
And that was the first Khan Academy,

00:14:40.100 --> 00:14:41.840
I had to put it on a website some place

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:45.900
and the Khan Academy
domain name was available.

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:47.650
I looked at stuff like Math Planet.

00:14:49.526 --> 00:14:51.200
But it was kind of
almost just a funny thing

00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:53.220
'cause it was just like me and my cousins.

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:55.640
And I kind of dreamt that
maybe one day other people

00:14:55.640 --> 00:14:58.870
could use this stuff and
it was actually a friend,

00:14:58.870 --> 00:15:00.770
it had nothing to do
with videos initially,

00:15:00.770 --> 00:15:03.770
and a friend said, "How are
you scaling your lessons?"

00:15:03.770 --> 00:15:06.860
And I said, "I'm not, it's hard to do,"

00:15:06.860 --> 00:15:08.790
at that point I had 15 cousins

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:10.540
that I was able to do with Nadia

00:15:10.540 --> 00:15:12.497
and he says, "Why don't
you make some videos

00:15:12.497 --> 00:15:14.520
"and upload them onto
YouTube for your family?"

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:16.150
And I immediately thought
that was a horrible idea,

00:15:16.150 --> 00:15:18.170
I said, "YouTube's for cats playing piano,

00:15:18.170 --> 00:15:19.670
'not serious math,"

00:15:19.670 --> 00:15:20.820
many people have heard me say that,

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:23.250
but that was the actual interchange.

00:15:23.250 --> 00:15:25.890
It was either that or dogs on skateboards,

00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:26.990
but you get the idea.

00:15:26.990 --> 00:15:27.823
- Yeah, yeah.

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:33.070
- But I got over the idea
that it wasn't my idea

00:15:33.070 --> 00:15:34.790
which is an important thing in life.

00:15:34.790 --> 00:15:39.240
And I gave it a shot
and those YouTube videos

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:41.760
started to get watched by
people who weren't my family

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:45.640
and one thing led to another and by 2010,

00:15:45.640 --> 00:15:48.050
2009, I had trouble focusing on my day job

00:15:48.050 --> 00:15:50.220
because there was about 100
000 people who were using

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:51.840
this thing called Khan Academy.

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:55.490
And so 2008 I had set it
up as a not for profit

00:15:55.490 --> 00:15:57.330
and actually at the time I was hoping,

00:15:57.330 --> 00:15:59.120
if I could really well
in the investment world

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:00.920
maybe one day in 10 years, 20 years,

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:03.300
I would have enough money
to be able to retire

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:04.530
and do this,

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:06.920
but then events happened
faster than I expected

00:16:06.920 --> 00:16:09.500
and I didn't have anywhere
near enough money.

00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:11.450
I was saving up money for
a down payment on our house

00:16:11.450 --> 00:16:13.220
and our first child had been born

00:16:13.220 --> 00:16:18.160
but you know, the zeitgeist of the world,

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:22.620
something was making me do this.

00:16:22.620 --> 00:16:24.850
But yeah, and quit my job,

00:16:24.850 --> 00:16:26.780
took a while to get some
of that first funding

00:16:26.780 --> 00:16:27.900
and then we're still raising money

00:16:27.900 --> 00:16:30.130
that's why we're here
trying to raise $5000.

00:16:30.130 --> 00:16:33.350
I will do this order of
operations problem for you.

00:16:33.350 --> 00:16:36.930
And you can explain this to family members

00:16:36.930 --> 00:16:38.580
and impress co-workers

00:16:38.580 --> 00:16:41.090
and people you meet in social gatherings.

00:16:41.090 --> 00:16:42.680
- Yeah like good holiday conversations.

00:16:42.680 --> 00:16:45.280
- Yeah I think if you walk up to someone

00:16:45.280 --> 00:16:48.624
that you might find appealing
at a party or something.

00:16:48.624 --> 00:16:51.233
I mean I think, what's cooler than that?

00:16:54.280 --> 00:16:56.150
You might think order
of operations is boring

00:16:56.150 --> 00:16:58.217
but let me tell you.

00:16:58.217 --> 00:17:00.810
(both laughing

00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:03.020
But anyway, that's how Khan Academy,

00:17:03.020 --> 00:17:06.560
that's how, and that
was back in 2009, 2010

00:17:06.560 --> 00:17:08.470
and we've just been on
a ride together since.

00:17:08.470 --> 00:17:11.870
And to be clear, Khan Academy
is much more than me now,

00:17:11.870 --> 00:17:13.950
you see some great people in this room

00:17:13.950 --> 00:17:18.100
and then there's 200
people outside of this room

00:17:18.100 --> 00:17:20.210
who help forward Khan Academy's mission,

00:17:20.210 --> 00:17:24.570
your donations helps us
have a team that can do this

00:17:24.570 --> 00:17:26.240
and there's actually
thousands of volunteers

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:27.930
on top of that.

00:17:27.930 --> 00:17:30.840
- Like engineers and content creators,

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:32.320
marketers, fund raisers.

00:17:32.320 --> 00:17:36.880
- Yeah, educators, teachers,
we have a thousand.

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:39.840
With your donation, just so people know,

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:41.610
we have to spend many millions of dollars

00:17:41.610 --> 00:17:44.680
just to keep the servers
going on Khan Academy.

00:17:44.680 --> 00:17:47.890
So it's a real operation.

00:17:47.890 --> 00:17:49.460
But in the course of the next few minutes

00:17:49.460 --> 00:17:51.700
if we can raise $5000.

00:17:51.700 --> 00:17:53.463
- Yeah that would be awesome.

00:17:53.463 --> 00:17:54.760
- Order of operations.
- And then we'll do

00:17:54.760 --> 00:17:55.705
some math problems.

00:17:55.705 --> 00:17:56.538
- Order of operations problems.

00:17:56.538 --> 00:17:57.371
- Awesome.

00:17:57.371 --> 00:17:58.760
So we have some more
questions that came in.

00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:00.290
- And we have donations too.

00:18:00.290 --> 00:18:01.123
- You want to talk about those?

00:18:01.123 --> 00:18:02.994
- Ang Lee thank you very much, Ang.

00:18:02.994 --> 00:18:06.160
And Heather Di Diego, thank you so much,

00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:08.923
donated on YouTube, she
found the blue button.

00:18:08.923 --> 00:18:11.113
- That's awesome, awesome.

00:18:11.113 --> 00:18:13.713
Cool, so what is your favorite subject?

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:17.990
- My favorite subject.

00:18:17.990 --> 00:18:22.330
You know, it's changed over time

00:18:22.330 --> 00:18:26.270
and it's really, it's
kind of whatever I'm into

00:18:26.270 --> 00:18:27.453
at the moment.

00:18:28.370 --> 00:18:30.380
I would say when I was growing up,

00:18:30.380 --> 00:18:32.680
my favorite subjects

00:18:32.680 --> 00:18:36.460
were probably people wouldn't be surprised

00:18:36.460 --> 00:18:37.810
to hear math and science

00:18:37.810 --> 00:18:41.850
but also I was really into art growing up,

00:18:41.850 --> 00:18:44.074
so those are my favorite subjects.

00:18:44.074 --> 00:18:45.750
I actually thought I was
going to be a cartoonist

00:18:45.750 --> 00:18:47.810
when I was, so obviously with the videos

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:51.610
I found an outlet to sketch for people.

00:18:51.610 --> 00:18:55.040
But now, through Khan Academy
we've worked on many subjects

00:18:55.040 --> 00:18:58.360
and I think the key is realizing
that almost every subject

00:18:58.360 --> 00:19:01.520
is actually really really
really interesting.

00:19:01.520 --> 00:19:04.930
Someone on, I think it was
on Instagram had just asked,

00:19:04.930 --> 00:19:07.090
how do I get the motivation to study?

00:19:07.090 --> 00:19:10.350
And for me it's, 'cause I have
to study for Khan Academy,

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:13.380
in fact, I'm one of the few
people over the age of 40

00:19:13.380 --> 00:19:15.970
who has to study the same
things that many of y'all

00:19:15.970 --> 00:19:18.290
have to study to make sure that I.

00:19:18.290 --> 00:19:21.950
But I don't want to study to just like

00:19:21.950 --> 00:19:23.410
be able to go through the motions

00:19:23.410 --> 00:19:24.850
or just to be able to get an answer right.

00:19:24.850 --> 00:19:27.200
I want to study to be able to appreciate

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:28.940
the mystery of the universe.

00:19:28.940 --> 00:19:30.650
And what you've got to
realize is a lot of the stuff

00:19:30.650 --> 00:19:31.770
that you might see in a textbook

00:19:31.770 --> 00:19:33.150
or that you might see on Khan Academy

00:19:33.150 --> 00:19:34.163
or other places,

00:19:35.070 --> 00:19:37.017
you might view it as,
"I've gotta figure out

00:19:37.017 --> 00:19:38.937
"how this equation works,
or I've got to figure out

00:19:38.937 --> 00:19:41.000
"what entropy means," or this or that.

00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:45.750
But you should be excited
because for example,

00:19:45.750 --> 00:19:46.820
notions of entropy,

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:48.520
these are things that people have pondered

00:19:48.520 --> 00:19:50.790
for hundreds of thousands of years

00:19:50.790 --> 00:19:53.640
and some people dedicated
their entire careers

00:19:53.640 --> 00:19:55.250
to some aspect of it

00:19:55.250 --> 00:19:57.460
and you're able to open up a
textbook or click on a video

00:19:57.460 --> 00:19:59.163
and essentially get the answer.

00:19:59.163 --> 00:20:02.010
And in some ways it spoils it

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:05.420
that you didn't struggle with it that long

00:20:05.420 --> 00:20:07.920
but you should get excited in that way.

00:20:07.920 --> 00:20:11.370
Think about how someone, Issac Newton

00:20:11.370 --> 00:20:13.370
would've given anything to get his hands

00:20:14.776 --> 00:20:16.566
on all of your textbooks.

00:20:16.566 --> 00:20:18.110
- Right.

00:20:18.110 --> 00:20:20.451
- Anyone, even a hundred years ago

00:20:20.451 --> 00:20:25.010
would give their arm to get
their hands on your textbooks,

00:20:25.010 --> 00:20:26.880
Khan Academy, et cetera, et cetera,

00:20:26.880 --> 00:20:28.590
because there's just so
much knowledge in it.

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:29.800
So when you have that mindset

00:20:29.800 --> 00:20:33.820
any subject becomes really
really really interesting

00:20:33.820 --> 00:20:36.490
and for me I love to look at subjects

00:20:36.490 --> 00:20:38.470
and start to realize connections

00:20:38.470 --> 00:20:39.840
that I had never seen before.

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:43.550
So I've actually recently
really gotten into etymology,

00:20:43.550 --> 00:20:46.810
because when you look at
where words comes from

00:20:46.810 --> 00:20:49.380
it starts to actually even explain a lot.

00:20:49.380 --> 00:20:51.351
One of my favorite ones,

00:20:51.351 --> 00:20:55.100
if this doesn't impress
people at social gatherings

00:20:55.100 --> 00:20:56.700
what I'm about to tell you will.

00:20:57.730 --> 00:21:00.583
I was talking about Eastern
philosophy and the Vedas,

00:21:00.583 --> 00:21:03.950
the Vedas are 3500 or older year old texts

00:21:03.950 --> 00:21:07.780
that are the basis of
Hinduism and Hindu philosophy.

00:21:07.780 --> 00:21:10.840
And in the Veda, the most
mentioned god is Indra,

00:21:10.840 --> 00:21:14.740
who is kind of the sky god
and he has aspects of Thor

00:21:15.620 --> 00:21:17.260
in Nordic mythology.

00:21:17.260 --> 00:21:20.750
And Indra's father's mentioned
in the Vedas 10 times

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:25.750
as sky father and sky father
is Dyeus Pather in Sanskrit.

00:21:27.150 --> 00:21:32.150
Dyeus Pather, Jupiter, Zeus Pater,

00:21:32.210 --> 00:21:37.210
so Jupiter, Zeus Pater and Dyeus Pather

00:21:38.490 --> 00:21:41.230
are all essentially and this
was one of the first pieces

00:21:41.230 --> 00:21:44.800
of evidence when the British came to India

00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:47.547
and they got some of the
Hindu priests to say,

00:21:47.547 --> 00:21:49.090
"Hey, teach us Sanskrit."

00:21:49.090 --> 00:21:49.923
That was the first time

00:21:49.923 --> 00:21:51.050
that they realized all these connections

00:21:51.050 --> 00:21:52.080
between all of these languages.

00:21:52.080 --> 00:21:53.250
It actually wasn't as obvious

00:21:53.250 --> 00:21:54.740
that even the Germanic languages

00:21:54.740 --> 00:21:58.030
and the Latin based languages
were so closely connected.

00:21:58.030 --> 00:22:01.350
When they saw Sanskrit in
India is so closely tied

00:22:01.350 --> 00:22:04.490
to these languages, it in
some ways blew everyone's mind

00:22:04.490 --> 00:22:05.323
and they started realizing

00:22:05.323 --> 00:22:07.560
wow, there must've been a people,

00:22:07.560 --> 00:22:10.700
a lot of Western historians would say,

00:22:10.700 --> 00:22:14.100
people who originated in central
Asian out of the Caucasus.

00:22:14.100 --> 00:22:16.980
A lot of Indians would say
maybe out of even North India,

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:19.990
but you share this common ancestry.

00:22:19.990 --> 00:22:21.010
Things like that, at least for me,

00:22:21.010 --> 00:22:22.160
give me chills

00:22:22.160 --> 00:22:24.559
because you're like
wow, there's like this,

00:22:24.559 --> 00:22:26.770
just over the last three,
four thousand years

00:22:26.770 --> 00:22:27.680
the things that have happened.

00:22:27.680 --> 00:22:32.270
And humanity, modern humans have been,

00:22:32.270 --> 00:22:33.730
physiologically modern humans

00:22:33.730 --> 00:22:35.800
have been around for several
hundreds of thousands of years,

00:22:35.800 --> 00:22:38.510
so just to even think about
all the things we don't know,

00:22:38.510 --> 00:22:40.700
all the stories.

00:22:40.700 --> 00:22:41.820
That kind of stuff gives me chills.

00:22:41.820 --> 00:22:44.370
So that's the kind of
thing when I was a kid,

00:22:44.370 --> 00:22:46.880
I was always interested by epic stories

00:22:46.880 --> 00:22:48.440
but sometimes if I was sitting

00:22:48.440 --> 00:22:51.070
in my 7th grade history
class, I wasn't as interested.

00:22:51.070 --> 00:22:53.180
But when I started to
view history like this

00:22:53.180 --> 00:22:54.620
then it all became super interesting

00:22:54.620 --> 00:22:57.271
so long answer to my favorite subject

